  steps: [
    { math: '1 · The information-processing frame', note: 'Cognition as stages: encode → store → retrieve, with attention as the bottleneck. The computer metaphor (1950s→) built the field — useful, incomplete (brains are embodied, emotional and parallel).' },
    { math: '2 · Working memory models', note: 'Baddeley\'s model: a central executive plus slave systems (phonological loop for sound, visuospatial sketchpad, episodic buffer). Predicts dual-task interference — why you can\'t read and listen to words at once.' },
    { math: '3 · Memory as reconstruction', note: 'Remembering rebuilds from fragments plus schemas — which is why memories drift, false memories implant ( Loftus\'s "lost in the mall" studies) and eyewitness confidence ≠ accuracy.' },
    { math: '4 · Dual-process theories', note: 'Two systems: fast, automatic, intuitive (System 1) vs slow, effortful, deliberate (System 2) — Kahneman\'s framing. The behavioural-economics lesson\'s biases are System 1\'s fingerprints on economic choices.' },
