<TopicPage slug="subjects/psychology/advanced/cognitive-psychology-and-models">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Cognitive psychology models <strong>the mind's information-handling: attention, memory,
    language, reasoning</strong> — with experiments precise enough to distinguish theories. It is
    the discipline where the computer metaphor was born, the place memory became reconstruction,
    and the source of the dual-process ideas that now run through economics and AI.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four frameworks that organise cognitive psychology" widget="equationStep" config={cognCfg} />

  <h2>Classic experiments as instruments</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Stroop (1935):</strong> naming the ink colour of the word "red" written in blue — the interference reveals automatic processing. Still the fastest attention test in the arsenal.</li>
    <li><strong>Sternberg (1966):</strong> reaction time grows linearly with memory-set size — a signature of serial scanning. The paradigm that made reaction time a window into mental steps.</li>
    <li><strong>Gorillas and change blindness:</strong> attention's narrow beam (the perception lesson) — cognition studies what the mind chooses to encode.</li>
    <li><strong>Cognitive neuroscience merger:</strong> models now get tested with brain imaging (the neuroscience ladder) — cognitive psychology became cognitive neuroscience in all but name.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Baddeley, Working Memory, Thought and Action; Kahneman, Thinking Fast and Slow; Loftus & Palmer (1974).</p>
</TopicPage>
